Now, we briefly motivate the representations in \figurename s~\ref{f:CH123} and~\ref{f:CH}.
Consider first $\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)$.
By \eqref{e:CH1} we have $\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+) \subseteq \mathsf{D}\times\{0\}$.
By \eqref{e:SleepToken}, we obtain
\[
u_{\ell} \in \pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right)
\iff
\overline{Q}\left(u_\ell\right) \in [q_*^-,q_*^+]
\iff
\left\{\begin{array}{@{}l@{\quad\hbox{if }}l@{}}
q_{\ell} \in [q_*^-,q_*^+] & v(u_{\ell}) \geqslant a,
\\
\overline{q}(u_{\ell}) \in [q_*^-,q_*^+] & v(u_{\ell}) < a.
\end{array}\right.
\]
As a consequence, on the one hand we have
\[
\pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right) \cap \{u \in \Omega : v(u) \geqslant a\} =
\{u \in \Omega : v(u) \geqslant a,\ q \in  [q_*^-,q_*^+]\}.
\]
On the other hand, observe that if $v(u_{\ell}) < a$ then the state on $\mathsf{FL}_1^{u_{\ell}}$ with maximum $q$-coordinate lies on the sonic line $q=a\,\rho$.
Hence, if $u_{\ell} \in \pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right)$ and $v(u_{\ell}) < a$, then $\overline{u}(u_{\ell})$ lies on the portion of the sonic line $q=a\,\rho \in [q_*^-,q_*^+]$, and therefore
\[\pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right) \cap \{u \in \Omega : v(u) < a\} = \bigcup_{q\in[q_*^-,q_*^+]} \left\{ u\in\mathsf{BL}_{1}^{(q/a,q)} : v(u)<a\right\}.
\]
We deduce that $\pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{1}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right)$ is as in \figurename~\ref{f:CH123}, left, where $u_{*a}^{\pm}$ are the intersections of the sonic line $q=a\,\rho$ with the horizontal lines $q=q_{*}^{\pm}$, respectively, namely $u_{*a}^{\pm} \doteq \left(q_{*}^{\pm}/a,q_{*}^{\pm}\right)$.
The representation of $\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{2}(q_*^-)$ follows from analogous arguments, see \eqref{e:CH2}.

We consider now $\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{3}(q_*^+)$ given in \eqref{e:CH3}.
Clearly, we have $v\left(\hat{u}(0,u_{\ell})\right) = 0 < a$; therefore, by \eqref{e:SleepToken}, if $u_{\ell} \in \pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{3}(q_*^+)\right)$ then $\overline{u}(\hat{u}(0,u_{\ell}))$ lies on the portion of the sonic line $q=a\,\rho > q_*^+$, namely,
\[u_{\ell} \in \pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}_{3}(q_*^+)\right)
\iff
\hat{u}(0,u_{\ell}) \in \bigcup_{q>q_*^+} \left\{ (\rho,q)\in\mathsf{BL}_{1}^{(q/a,q)} : q=0\right\}
\iff
u_{\ell} \in \bigcup_{\rho>\rho_{*0}^+} \mathsf{BL}_{1}^{(\rho,0)},
\]
where we recall that $u_{*0}^+$ is the unique element of $\mathsf{BL}_{1}^{u_{*a}^+}$ with $q=0$.
At last, the above considerations lead to the representation of $\pi_{\ell}\left(\mathsf{CH}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right)$ as in \figurename~\ref{f:CH} and to \eqref{e:incodom2}.

\label{p:area}
There exist constants $C_1,C_2>0$, independent of $q_*^\pm$, and
a function $A$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{e:keeel}
\left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,0}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^+)\right| = \frac{C_1}{a} \, (q_*^+)^2 \quad \hbox{ and }\quad
\left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right| =
\frac{C_2}{a} \, A\left(\frac{q_*^-}{q_*^+}\right)(q_*^+)^2.
\end{equation}
The function $A$ is strictly increasing, strictly convex and $A(0)=0$, see \eqref{e:A} for its explicit expression; its graph is plotted in \figurename~\ref{f:A}.
In particular, the map $[0,q_*] \ni q_{*}^- \mapsto \left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right|$ is strictly increasing, the map $[q_*,+\infty) \ni q_{*}^+ \mapsto \left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right|$ is strictly decreasing and
\begin{align}
&\lim_{q_{*}^- \to 0} \left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right| = 0,&
&\lim_{q_{*}^+ \to +\infty} \left|\mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+)\right| = 0.
\label{e:kkwj}
\end{align}

\label{ex:1}
Consider the initial datum $u_\ell =(2.5,2)$, $u_r=(5,-2)$, and $h_o=1$, so that the valve is initially open. In this case $v(u_\ell)<a$, implying that $\overline{Q}(u_\ell) = \overline{q}(u_\ell) \approx 2.7441 \in [q_*^-,q_*^+]$. Therefore $h_{\rm c}(u_\ell,h_o)=h_o=1$ and $q_{\rm c}^{0}(u_\ell,h_o) = \overline{q}(u_\ell)$. 
It follows then that $\mathcal{RS}_{\rm c}[u_\ell,u_r]$ consists of a $1$-rarefaction connecting the left state $u_\ell$ to $u_{\rm c}^- \approx (1.3729,2.7441)$, a stationary c-wave connecting $u_{\rm c}^-$ to $u_{\rm c}^+ \approx (7.3440,2.7441)$, and a $2$-shock connecting $u_{\rm c}^+$ to the right state $u_r$, see \figurename~\ref{fig:ex1}.

\label{ex:2}
As in Example~\ref{ex:1}, consider the initial states $u_\ell =(2.5,2)$, $u_r=(5,-2)$, but now assume that the valve is initially closed, namely $h_o=0$. As already observed in the previous example, we have $v(u_\ell) < a$, implying that $\overline{Q}(u_\ell) = \overline{q}(u_\ell) \approx 2.7441 \in [q_*^-,q_*^+]$. Therefore, in this case we have $h_{\rm c}(u_\ell,h_o)=h_o=0$ and $q_{\rm c}^{0}(u_\ell,h_o) = 0$. 
It follows then that $\mathcal{RS}_{\rm c}[u_\ell,u_r]$ consists of a $1$-shock connecting the left state $u_\ell$ to $u_{\rm c}^- \approx (3.7198,0)$, a stationary c-wave connecting $u_{\rm c}^-$ to $u_{\rm c}^+ \approx (6.1050,0)$, and a $2$-shock connecting $u_{\rm c}^+$ to the right state $u_r$, see \figurename~\ref{fig:ex2}.

\label{ex:4}
Consider the initial states $u_\ell=(2.5,-4)$ and $u_r = (3,3)$. In this case $v(u_\ell) < a$, and therefore we have $\overline{Q}(u_\ell) = \overline{q}(u_\ell) \approx 0.8265 <q_*^-$.
As a result, the valve closes for $t>0$, $h_{\rm c}(u_\ell,h_o)=0$, yielding $q_{\rm c}^{0}(u_\ell,h_o)=0$, independently of the initial value of $h_o$. The left state $u_\ell$ is then connected to $u_{\rm c}^-\approx(1.1233,0)$ with a $1$-rarefaction, $u_{\rm c}^-$ to $u_{\rm c}^+\approx(1.8196,0)$ with a c-wave, and $u_{\rm c}^+$ to $u_r$ with a $2$-rarefaction, see \figurename~\ref{fig:ex4}.

\label{ex:CHc1}
Consider the initial states $u_\ell=(0.18,1.95)$ and $u_r = (1,0.5).$ As $v(u_\ell)>a$, then $\overline{Q}(u_\ell) = q_\ell = 1.95 < q_*^-$ meaning that $q_{\rm c}^{0}(u_\ell,h_o) =0$ both for $h_o=0$ and $h_o=1$; moreover, $u_\ell \in \mathsf{CH}_{\ell,1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^-,q_*^+) \subset \mathsf{CH}_{\ell,0}^{\scriptscriptstyle\complement}(q_*^+)$. As a result, {\em independently of the initial value of $h_o$} we get the same numerical solution; such a solution is displayed in \figurename~\ref{fig:chattering1} and exhibits chattering effect. It can be observed that $q_{j_{\rm c}}^n=q_\ell$ after a finite number of time steps, because $q_\ell < q_*^-$. On the other hand, we have $q_{j_{\rm c}+1}^n \neq q_\ell$ because by construction $q_{j_{\rm c}+1}^n$ belongs to $\{0\} \cup [q_*^-,q_*]$ whereas $q_\ell$ does not; therefore the approximate solution does not satisfy the first Rankine-Hugoniot condition \eqref{e:RH1} at $x=0$.
In other words, the scheme does not conserve the mass in this case!
